course: dictionary
lesson: 7080
title: Pave and its two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pave is a verb. To pave a road, path or yard is to cover it with a hard, flat surface such as stones, bricks, concrete or tarmac. «Workers pave the driveway» tells you they make the ground smooth and hard so that cars and bikes can use it.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Paving stone» and «paved with» both point to something hard and flat laid on the ground. Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word: "cover the road with a hard flat surface" still makes sense, so you know you have it right.
explain: Pave has a second meaning too. «Pave the way» means to make it easier for something to happen later. When inventors «paved the way» for others, their work made things easier for the people who came after. No stones are involved, so the sentence around the word tells you which meaning is meant.
fact: Pave means to cover a road, path or yard with a hard, flat surface.
fact: Materials used for paving include stones, bricks, concrete and tarmac.
fact: A «paving stone» or «paving slab» is a flat piece used to cover a path or yard.
fact: «Pave the way» means to make it easier for something to happen later.
fact: «The Romans built about 80,000 kilometres of straight, paved roads», and many modern roads still follow Roman routes.
exercise: «Workers pave the driveway with smooth concrete.» What does pave mean here? | Sweep away the leaves | Dig a deep hole in | Paint in bright colours | *Cover with a hard, flat surface
exercise: «Inventors paved the way for others.» What does «paved the way» mean? | Painted a path red | Built a stone wall around the town | Walked very slowly along a path | *Made it easier for others to follow later
exercise: A yard is 6 m long and 4 m wide. Each slab is 1 m square. How many slabs are needed to pave the whole yard? | 20 | 30 | 10 | *24
test: «Roots can lift a paving stone.» What is a paving stone? | A tall stone tower | *A flat stone used to cover a path | A very soft stone for carving | A stone that floats on water
test: In «pave a road», which word is closest in meaning to pave? | Dig | *Cover | Break | Paint
exam: «The Romans paved roads so their armies could move fast.» Why did the Romans pave their roads? | To make the roads wet and slippery | *So armies and messengers could travel quickly | To hide from enemies | To grow trees along the sides
